--TEST--
Core\Encoding::encodeText names the first character the charset cannot spell where iconv() answers false, drops it, or approximates it
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`iconv` has three answers for a character the target charset has no spelling for, and a ported program has picked one of them by suffix. Plain `iconv("UTF-8", "ASCII", "café")` returns **`false`** and raises a `Notice`, and `false` is the value that then flows on — concatenated it is the empty string, so the octets a caller believed they had converted are simply absent from what they write out. `//IGNORE` **drops** the character: `iconv("UTF-8", "ISO-8859-1//IGNORE", "a☃b")` is `"ab"`, two octets where three characters went in, with nothing in the result to say a third existed. `//TRANSLIT` **approximates** it where the C library has an approximation and returns `false` where it has none — on this box `é` becomes `'e` and `☃` fails outright, so the mode is not even reliably lossy-but-total, and *which* approximation arrives is a property of the platform's iconv rather than of the program. All three lose the one thing a caller needs to act: which character, and where. `Core\Encoding::encodeText` has one answer and it is the throw. It is a `Fault::thrown` naming the member, the `Core\Charset` case that refused, the character, its code point and its byte offset in the subject, catchable like any other `Throwable` (ADR 0002), and there is no `//IGNORE` and no `//TRANSLIT` to write — that is ADR 0009 § 3 and R4, and `nvs_stdlib::encoding`'s own module doc is where the rule lives. This is AGENTS.md's priority ordering at 2 over 4: a `?`, a `&#8364;` or a silently shorter string is a wrong answer that runs, and it surfaces at whatever reads the octets back rather than at the conversion that made them. A caller who genuinely wants a lossy spelling writes the replacement they want, in their own text, where a reader of the program can see it — the last two rows below are exactly `//IGNORE` and `//TRANSLIT` said out loud, and they are what makes this a demand for the spelling rather than a capability Novis lacks. The agreeing half — every encoding both implementations carry, over text each of them can spell — is asserted against PHP itself in `encoding-encode-text-matches-iconv`.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Refusal {
    // What a caller needs from the throw is the three facts `iconv` reports
    // through none of its three modes: which charset refused, which character
    // it has no spelling for, and where that character was. The wording
    // around them is `nvs_stdlib::encoding`'s own and is not what these rows
    // are about.
    public static function names(string $message, string $case, string $point, string $offset): string {
        bool $ok = Core\Str::startsWith($message, "Core\\Encoding::encodeText(): ")
            && Core\Str::contains($message, "Core\\Charset::" . $case)
            && Core\Str::contains($message, "(U+" . $point . ")")
            && Core\Str::contains($message, "at offset " . $offset);
        return $ok as string;
    }
}

uint $refused = 0;

// The row plain `iconv` answers `false` for, and the one a ported program is
// most likely to be carrying: a Latin-1 character on its way into ASCII.
try { Core\Encoding::encodeText("café au lait", Core\Charset::Ascii); }
catch (Throwable $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "ascii|", Refusal::names($e->message, "Ascii", "00E9", "3"), "\n"; }

// The row `//IGNORE` drops and `//TRANSLIT` also fails on, since the C library
// has no approximation for a snowman. The offset is counted in the subject's
// own octets, so a character that is three bytes in UTF-8 is still reported at
// where it starts rather than at which character it is.
try { Core\Encoding::encodeText("a☃b", Core\Charset::Latin1); }
catch (Throwable $snow) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "latin1|", Refusal::names($snow->message, "Latin1", "2603", "1"), "\n"; }

// A multi-byte table refuses on the same terms as a single-byte one: Shift_JIS
// spells both kana and the character before the euro sign, and the refusal is
// still about the one character it cannot spell rather than about the string.
try { Core\Encoding::encodeText("あ€ア", Core\Charset::ShiftJis); }
catch (Throwable $jis) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "shiftjis|", Refusal::names($jis->message, "ShiftJis", "20AC", "3"), "\n"; }

// The first refused character and not the first non-ASCII one: `žluť` is four
// characters ISO-8859-2 spells perfectly well, and what stops the conversion
// is the euro sign seven octets in.
try { Core\Encoding::encodeText("žluť €", Core\Charset::Iso88592); }
catch (Throwable $cz) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "iso88592|", Refusal::names($cz->message, "Iso88592", "20AC", "7"), "\n"; }

// Counted rather than read off the lines above: a charset that stopped
// refusing fails here as well as shifting its own row.
echo "refused=", $refused, "/4\n";

// `//IGNORE`, written out. The removal is in the caller's own text, where a
// reader can see that two of the three characters are what gets written.
echo Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Encoding::encodeText(Core\Str::replace("a☃b", "☃", ""), Core\Charset::Latin1)), "\n";

// `//TRANSLIT`, written out — and the approximation is the caller's rather
// than the platform's, which is the half of that mode no program could pin.
echo Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Encoding::encodeText(Core\Str::replace("café au lait", "é", "e"), Core\Charset::Ascii)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
ascii|1
latin1|1
shiftjis|1
iso88592|1
refused=4/4
6162
63616665206175206c616974
